--TEST--
A writer refuses a call out of its own state rather than emitting what a parser would refuse, and a refusal writes nothing — `rule:core-classes/xml-tree-and-stream`, `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// What is open is the writer's, not the caller's, so every sentence below is
// one the writer reached by looking at itself. None of them writes a byte: the
// document echoed at the end is exactly what the accepted calls put in it.
Core\Xml\Writer $w = Core\Xml::writer();

// The document is the outer pair, so everything else is inside it.
try {
    $w->startElement("order");
} catch (LogicError $closed) {
    echo $closed->message, "\n";
}

$w->startDocument();
try {
    $w->startDocument();
} catch (LogicError $twice) {
    echo $twice->message, "\n";
}

// Character data belongs inside an element, and closing needs something open.
try {
    $w->content("loose");
} catch (LogicError $loose) {
    echo $loose->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $w->cdata("loose");
} catch (LogicError $loose) {
    echo $loose->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $w->endElement();
} catch (LogicError $nothing) {
    echo $nothing->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $w->endDocument();
} catch (LogicError $empty) {
    echo $empty->message, "\n";
}

// The three constructs XML gives no escape grammar to are refused rather than
// rewritten, because rewriting them would be a guess about what was meant.
try {
    $w->comment("a -- b");
} catch (LogicError $dashes) {
    echo $dashes->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $w->instruction("xml", "version=\"1.0\"");
} catch (LogicError $reserved) {
    echo $reserved->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $w->doctype("1st");
} catch (LogicError $digit) {
    echo $digit->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $w->attribute("k", "v");
} catch (LogicError $unattached) {
    echo $unattached->message, "\n";
}

// An attribute goes on the start tag it belongs to, once, and only while that
// tag is still open.
$w->startElement("order");
$w->attribute("id", "7");
try {
    $w->attribute("id", "8");
} catch (LogicError $repeated) {
    echo $repeated->message, "\n";
}
$w->content("one");
try {
    $w->attribute("late", "9");
} catch (LogicError $late) {
    echo $late->message, "\n";
}
$w->endElement();

echo $w->endDocument(), "\n";

// And it is answered once: a writer is not a buffer a second call reads back.
try {
    $w->endDocument();
} catch (LogicError $again) {
    echo $again->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Xml\Writer::startElement(): the document is not open, and `startDocument` is what opens it.
Core\Xml\Writer::startDocument(): the document is already open.
Core\Xml\Writer::content(): character data belongs inside an element, and none is open.
Core\Xml\Writer::cdata(): character data belongs inside an element, and none is open.
Core\Xml\Writer::endElement(): no element is open, so there is nothing to close.
Core\Xml\Writer::endDocument(): a document has a root element, and none was written.
Core\Xml\Writer::comment(): a comment has no escape grammar, so it cannot hold `--` or end with `-`.
Core\Xml\Writer::instruction(): `xml` is the declaration's own target, which `startDocument` writes.
Core\Xml\Writer::doctype(): `1st` is not a name a document can write.
Core\Xml\Writer::attribute(): no start tag is still taking attributes — an attribute goes on its element before anything is written inside it.
Core\Xml\Writer::attribute(): this element already carries an attribute named `id`.
Core\Xml\Writer::attribute(): no start tag is still taking attributes — an attribute goes on its element before anything is written inside it.
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><order id="7">one</order>
Core\Xml\Writer::endDocument(): the document is finished, and `endDocument` was already called.
